's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=4 \\\\ 0=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-34364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96c38612cdde1be4c847596728e163a1b542ae0ab3274c80438acf68df03b68"} {"question" "q-34364" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-34364" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Col}(E)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-34364" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "b\\notin \\text{Col}(E)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-111053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $M$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{m}_{1}+{x}_{2}{m}_{2}+{x}_{3}{m}_{3}+{x}_{4}{m}_{4},$ and ${m}_{1}$, ${m}_{2}$, ${m}_{3}$, and ${m}_{4}$ are respective columns of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 6 & -12\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-111053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=1 \\\\ 0=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=8.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(M).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-111053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96c38612cdde1be4c847596728e163a1b542ae0ab3274c80438acf68df03b68"} {"question" "q-111053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-111053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-15"] ["math" "-25"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Col}(M)."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-111053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "b\\notin \\text{Col}(M)."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3},$ and ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$, and ${c}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-(2) & =-12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-8-3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-8-3(-5)-3(2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-5)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=1\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 2=-10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96c38612cdde1be4c847596728e163a1b542ae0ab3274c80438acf68df03b68"} {"question" "q-110982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-15"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $M$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{m}_{1}+{x}_{2}{m}_{2}+{x}_{3}{m}_{3},$ and ${m}_{1}$, ${m}_{2}$, and ${m}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -24 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=8 \\\\ {x}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+(8) & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${m}_{1},$ ${m}_{2},$ and ${m}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{1}}+8\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=1\\cdot 8\\cdot 2=16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d96c38612cdde1be4c847596728e163a1b542ae0ab3274c80438acf68df03b68"} {"question" "q-110983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "23"] ["math" "34"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "q-110983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb1a6717ab693ec0afe7d43e2ce0cf3f4b1decbbda17833059c0d0a4db4e4d61"} {"question" "e-4807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $B$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. \nDetermine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(B).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the values of $x_1$ and $x_2,$ where $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{b}_1,$ $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are the respective columns of $B.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/assets/b8e5970e24616ddcb86603e266955cc15c7704796eead3f3596ba1f588bb0465.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/example-4807.json" "file_sha256" "d5a62c43af2d63b28348b48b022f034ada881c491a46e1e7ed57750ff585a702"} {"question" "e-4807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(B),$ we need to check if the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 15 & -6 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=7 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the true statement $0=0.$ So the system is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=7 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=0.$ Substituting into the first equation gives $x_1=\\dfrac 7 5.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(B).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/assets/6e24e4831759f4f4a8b24ec7b3c1c2c7cc44df6be36c5679d2ac8b33a17d8c5c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/example-4807.json" "file_sha256" "d5a62c43af2d63b28348b48b022f034ada881c491a46e1e7ed57750ff585a702"} {"question" "e-6208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix given by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/assets/7565496e57b9dede7d994f9e8ee936e158f67cc8c7397e182c9c6e940939f823.png) Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/assets/b3be9df23c26f562cd1e7c5e1162e87bcc7da0085c00b0185221a6ae45f11395.png) belongs to $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/example-6208.json" "file_sha256" "8800d8979dfc3478f8392584ca00f8eccdbfebc71ef2477760e5fd3840677dca"} {"question" "e-6208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2+k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2+k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k-48\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k-48\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+11{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=10 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 0=k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-4,$ which means that $k=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/example-6208.json" "file_sha256" "8800d8979dfc3478f8392584ca00f8eccdbfebc71ef2477760e5fd3840677dca"} {"question" "e-4808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $C$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(C).$ In affirmative case, find the value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3$, where $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{c}_1+x_2\\mathbf{c}_3+x_3\\mathbf{c}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_1,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/assets/ae098d868e48d331a3cf9f73fe0995eadb60c86e1a74eec039b9044592c55a5c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/example-4808.json" "file_sha256" "bff10be53f0e8ed5bc1f1f494eaf80b459f6ab810ddfb149599cbfbbb4100465"} {"question" "e-4808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $C\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3=1$. Substituting into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+4(1) & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-(-1)-4(1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $C\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{c}_1,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/assets/210497f8b94b3600ef3b784333c7388f65678cd18fb7fdd64f9cc14f0b37c87e.png)\n\nFinally, we get $x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3 = 2 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 = -2$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064257/example-4808.json" "file_sha256" "bff10be53f0e8ed5bc1f1f494eaf80b459f6ab810ddfb149599cbfbbb4100465"}] "usage" {"e-4808" [] "e-4807" [] "e-6208" []} "basis" 2100 "retractions" [[17592186119548 :question/problem "The matrix $B$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below.\nDetermine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(B)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$, where $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2$, and $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are the respective columns of $B$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 15 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119548 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(B)$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}B \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}B & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 15 & -6 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}↔R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 7; x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the true statement $0=0$. So the system is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 7; x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=0$. Substituting into the first equation gives\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 7 5\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(B)$.\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119594 :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$."] [17592186119594 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, then the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nmust be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}A \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{8}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 11R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 2x_{2} = 10; x_{2} = 4; 0 = k - 4\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-4$, which means that $k=4$."] [17592186119549 :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(C)$. In affirmative case, find the value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3$, where $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{c}_1+x_2\\mathbf{c}_3+x_3\\mathbf{c}_3$, and $\\mathbf{c}_1$, $\\mathbf{c}_2$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119549 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(C)$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nC\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}C \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}C & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 5; 3x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 1; 5x_{3} = 5\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3=1$. Substituting into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{2} + 4(1) &= 1 \\\\\n3x_{2} &= -3 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 5 - x_{2} - 4x_{3} \\\\\n&= 5 - (-1) - 4(1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation\n\n$$\nC\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(C)$.\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{c}_1$, $\\mathbf{c}_2$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3 = 2 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 = -2\n$$\n\n."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
